    {
      "id": "reader_unit_013",
      "section_kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "section_title": "Interpretive Questions",
      "text": "Prevents generic suffering praise from erasing causes, agency, consent, disability, treatment, economics, and accountability in each passage. Acts portrays Paul unable to see, led by others, fasting three days, visited and touched by Ananias, regaining sight, receiving baptism and food, and entering mission. Treats dependency and care as part of the call narrative without requiring cure before vocation or identifying the event as Paul's thorn. Acts narrates earlier mission events from a later composition horizon, while Corinthians, Galatians, and Philippians are earlier occasional self-reports with different selections and aims. Prevents Acts' blindness, recovery, stoning, or prison scenes from diagnosing the thorn, Galatian weakness, or every Pauline hardship. The selected texts distinguish low status, bodily incapacity, illness, injury, violence, deprivation, custody, rhetorical evaluation, and cross-shaped power.",
      "verse_refs": [
        "Acts 9:1-19"
      ],
